Oracle Database 19c on OEL10 using AutoUpgrade
***********************************************
From Multiple Downloads and Manual Patching to One Config File and One Java Command.

After reading Mike Dietrich’s article on Oracle Database 19c support for Oracle Linux 10 / RHEL 10, I wanted to validate the setup myself in a fresh Oracle Linux 10.2 environment.

Instead of following the traditional Oracle Database software installation and patching approach, I decided to take the test one step further and use AutoUpgrade to download the required software and patches, create the Oracle Home, and apply the patches automatically.

Can AutoUpgrade take care of downloading the Oracle software, downloading all required patches, building the Oracle Home, and applying those patches automatically?

The answer from my test was a clear YES.

That turned out to be the most interesting part of this lab.

Using a single AutoUpgrade configuration file and one Java command, I was able to download the Oracle Database 19.32 Gold Image together with the recommended patches. In my lab, the complete download took less than five minutes.

And what impressed me most was not simply that Oracle Database 19c worked successfully on Oracle Linux 10.
It was how much AutoUpgrade simplified the complete software download, Oracle Home creation, and patching workflow.

In my test, downloading the Oracle Database 19.32 Gold Image along with the recommended patches took less than five minutes.

******************************************************************************************************************

You can use AutoUpgrade to install an Oracle Home on a New Server


Environment

[oracle@caoradboel10 ~]$ uname -a
Linux caoradboel10 6.12.0-206.104.4.4.el10uek.x86_64 #2 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Wed Sep 16 22:08:45 PDT 2026 x86_64 GNU/Linux
[oracle@caoradboel10 ~]$ cat /etc/oracle-release
Oracle Linux Server release 10.2
[oracle@caoradboel10 ~]$


Pre-requisities

1. Install required RPMs for Oracle Installation as 'root' user

Normally, Oracle Linux database servers can be prepared using: oracle-database-preinstall-19c

The preinstallation RPM takes care of many operating-system prerequisites, including required packages and system configuration.
During my test on September 25, 2026, the oracle-database-preinstall-19c RPM was not available through the repository configuration I was using on OL10.
Therefore, I completed the prerequisite configuration manually.

Note: If the Oracle Database preinstallation RPM is available through your configured OL10 repositories, using it is simpler. The manual steps here document what I used in my lab environment.
 
Please refer to Oracles Doc regarding the Required packages for Oracle Linux 10
#https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/19/ladbi/supported-oracle-linux-10-distributions-for-x86-64.html

#Manual Setup
 
 dnf install -y bc 
 dnf install -y elfutils-libelf 
 dnf install -y fontconfig
 dnf install -y ksh
 dnf install -y libvirt-libs
 dnf install -y libXi
 dnf install -y libXtst
 dnf install -y libxcrypt-compat
 dnf install -y sysstat
 dnf install -y smartmontools
 dnf install -y xz-devel
 dnf install -y libnsl

 
 #I installed Java 21 for AutoUpgrade as well. The autoupgrade.jar requires Java 21
 dnf install -y java-21-openjdk-devel 
 
 #Which is required for AutoUpgrade to unzip the downloaded patches.
 dnf install -y zip
 
 #Additional package if you need GUI
 dnf install -y xterm
 
#Below packages were listed by Oracle required packages and they are part of OL10. There's no wrong if you try to install them except you will get a message "package is already installed."
 dnf install -y binutils
 dnf install -y glibc
 dnf install -y glibc-devel
 dnf install -y libaio
 dnf install -y libgcc
 dnf install -y libibverbs
 dnf install -y libstdc++
 dnf install -y libvirt-libs
 dnf install -y libxcb
 dnf install -y libX11
 dnf install -y libXau
 dnf install -y libXi
 dnf install -y libXrender
 dnf install -y libXtst
 dnf install -y libxcrypt-compat
 dnf install -y make
 dnf install -y policycoreutils
 dnf install -y policycoreutils-python-utils
 
2. Create new groups and oracle user for oracle installation

  groupadd -g 54321 oinstall
  groupadd -g 54322 dba
  groupadd -g 54323 oper
  groupadd -g 54324 backupdba
  groupadd -g 54325 dgdba
  groupadd -g 54326 kmdba
  groupadd -g 54327 asmdba
  groupadd -g 54328 asmoper
  groupadd -g 54329 asmadmin
  groupadd -g 54330 racdba
  
  useradd -u 54321 -g oinstall -G dba,oper oracle

3. Add the below lines to the "/etc/sysctl.conf" file

cd /etc/sysctl.d/
cp -prf /etc/sysctl.conf 99-initial-sysctl.conf
vi /etc/sysctl.conf

# Manual setting up of the kernel parameters as oracle-database-preinstall-19c is not available on oel10 as of Sep 25, 2026

fs.file-max = 6815744
kernel.sem = 250 32000 100 128
kernel.shmmni = 4096
kernel.shmall = 1073741824
kernel.shmmax = 4398046511104
kernel.panic_on_oops = 1
net.core.rmem_default = 262144
net.core.rmem_max = 4194304
net.core.wmem_default = 262144
net.core.wmem_max = 1048576
net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter = 2
net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter = 2
fs.aio-max-nr = 4194304
net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range = 9000 65535
vm.hugetlb_shm_group = 54321

#Run the below command to update the changes to the current kernel parameters

/sbin/sysctl -p

4. Add the below lines to the "/etc/security/limits.d/oracle-database-preinstall-19c.conf" file


# Manual setting up of the limits as oracle-database-preinstall-19c is not available on oel10 as of Sep 25, 2026
oracle   soft   nofile    1024
oracle   hard   nofile    65536
oracle   soft   nproc    16384
oracle   hard   nproc    16384
oracle   soft   stack    10240
oracle   hard   stack    32768
oracle   hard   memlock    134217728
oracle   soft   memlock    134217728
oracle   soft   data    unlimited
oracle   hard   data    unlimited

#End of Manual Setup

 
5. Setup the SELINUX to permissive or disable it

    vi /etc/selinux/config
	
6. Disable firewalld

    systemctl stop firewalld
    systemctl disable firewalld
	
